Position Based Fluids (PBF) is a Lagrangian particle-based fluid simulation method that enforces incompressibility by correcting particle positions based on density constraints, allowing real-time simulation without meshing. It distinguishes itself from Smoothed Particle Hydrodynamics (SPH) by treating fluid dynamics as a constraint satisfaction problem solved via iterative position projections rather than solving the Navier-Stokes equations through pressure gradients. This approach provides superior stability for incompressible flows and simplifies the handling of surface tension and boundary conditions. PBF simulates fluid as a collection of particles, each carrying position, velocity, and mass. The core mechanism involves predicting particle motion under external forces and then iteratively correcting positions to satisfy physical constraints (primarily density incompressibility and surface tension). Inputs: Particle positions \mathbf{p}i, velocities \mathbf{v}i, masses mi, rest density ho0, kernel radius r, time step \Delta t, external forces \mathbf{f}{ext}, and boundary definitions. Outputs: Updated particle positions \mathbf{p}i^{new} and velocities \mathbf{v}i^{new}. Particles: An array of structures containing: position: 3D vector (x, y, z). velocity: 3D vector (vx, vy, vz). mass: Scalar float. oldposition: 3D vector for velocity reconstruction. posdelta: 3D vector accumulating constraint corrections. gradnormsq: Scalar float accumulating gradient norms for the constraint denominator. Neighbor List: A spatial hash grid or brute-force search to efficiently find neighbors within kernel radius r for each particle. PBF uses the Cubic SPH kernel W(\mathbf{p}, r) and its gradient 
abla{\mathbf{p}} W(\mathbf{p}, r), where r is the kernel radius and q =  rac{\|\mathbf{p}\|}{r}. The kernel function is: W(\mathbf{p}, r) =  egin{cases}  rac{8}{\pi r^3} (6q^2(q-1)+1), & 0 \le q \le 0.5 \  rac{16}{\pi r^3} (1-q)^3, & 0.5 < q \le 1 \ 0, & 	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} The gradient of the kernel function is: 
abla{\mathbf{p}} W(\mathbf{p}, r) =  egin{cases}  rac{48}{\pi r^5} (3q-2)\mathbf{p}, & 0 \le \|\mathbf{p}\|/r \le 0.5 \ - rac{48}{\pi r^5} q \mathbf{p}, & 0.5 < \|\mathbf{p}\|/r \le 1 \ 0, & 	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} 1. Initialization: Initialize particle positions in a grid pattern within the domain using \delta = h 	imes 0.8 and boundary offsets. Initialize velocities with random values between -2 and 2. Set oldposition equal to current position. 2. Prediction Step: For each particle i, predict the new position 	ilde{\mathbf{p}}i using explicit Euler integration: 	ilde{\mathbf{p}}i = \mathbf{p}i + \Delta t \mathbf{v}i + \Delta t^2 \mathbf{a}i where \mathbf{a}i = \mathbf{f}{ext, i} / mi. 3. Constraint Solving (Iterative Projection): Initialize position deltas \Delta \mathbf{p}i = \mathbf{0} and gradient norms 	ext{grad
orm\sq}i = 0 for all particles. Repeat for pbfnumiters iterations: Density Constraint: For each particle i, compute the density constraint violation: C{iho} =  rac{1}{ho0} \sum{j \in 	ext{neighbors}(i)} mj W(\mathbf{p}i - \mathbf{p}j, r) - 1 where C{iho} = 0 indicates incompressibility. Compute the gradient of the constraint with respect to particle k (i or j): 
abla{\mathbf{p}k} C{iho} =  rac{mj}{ho0} 
abla{\mathbf{p}k} W(\mathbf{p}i - \mathbf{p}j, r) Solve for the Lagrange multiplier \Delta \lambdai using the XPBD-like update formula (assuming diagonal mass matrix approximation M{ii} = mi): \Delta \lambdai =  rac{-C{iho}}{\Delta t^2 \sum{k \in \{i, 	ext{neighbors}\}}  rac{1}{mk} \|
abla{\mathbf{p}k} C{iho}\|^2 + \alpha} where \alpha is a compliance parameter for XPBD. Update position deltas: \Delta \mathbf{p}k +=  rac{\Delta \lambdai}{mk} 
abla{\mathbf{p}k} C{iho} Finally divide \Delta \mathbf{p}i by ho0 as per implementation convention. Surface Tension (Optional): Detect surface particles using occlusion estimation: enclose particle in a spherical screen, project neighbors, and classify as surface if cumulative projection area is below a threshold. For surface particles, compute normal \mathbf{n}i = 	ext{normalize}(-
abla{\mathbf{p}i} C{iho}). Triangulate neighboring surface particles on the plane perpendicular to \mathbf{n}i. Compute Area Constraint C{iA}: C{iA} = \sum{t \in T(i)}  rac{1}{2} \|(\mathbf{p}{t2} - \mathbf{p}{t1}) 	imes (\mathbf{p}{t3} - \mathbf{p}{t1})\| where T(i) is the set of neighboring triangles. Compute Jacobians for area constraint vertices \mathbf{t}1, \mathbf{t}2, \mathbf{t}3: 
abla{\mathbf{t}1} C{tA} =  rac{(\mathbf{p}{t2} - \mathbf{p}{t1}) 	imes (\mathbf{p}{t3} - \mathbf{p}{t1}) 	imes (\mathbf{p}{t3} - \mathbf{p}{t2})}{2 \|(\mathbf{p}{t2} - \mathbf{p}{t1}) 	imes (\mathbf{p}{t3} - \mathbf{p}{t1})\|} 
abla{\mathbf{t}2} C{tA} =  rac{(\mathbf{p}{t3} - \mathbf{p}{t2}) 	imes (\mathbf{p}{t1} - \mathbf{p}{t2}) 	imes (\mathbf{p}{t1} - \mathbf{p}{t3})}{2 \|(\mathbf{p}{t2} - \mathbf{p}{t1}) 	imes (\mathbf{p}{t3} - \mathbf{p}{t1})\|} 
abla{\mathbf{t}3} C{tA} =  rac{(\mathbf{p}{t1} - \mathbf{p}{t3}) 	imes (\mathbf{p}{t2} - \mathbf{p}{t3}) 	imes (\mathbf{p}{t2} - \mathbf{p}{t1})}{2 \|(\mathbf{p}{t1} - \mathbf{p}{t3}) 	imes (\mathbf{p}{t2} - \mathbf{p}{t3})\|} Solve for area constraint multipliers and update position deltas similarly to density constraints. Distance Constraint (Optional): Enforce minimum distance d0 between particles: C{ij}^D = \min\{0, \|\mathbf{p}i - \mathbf{p}j\| - d0\} Compute gradients: 
abla{\mathbf{p}i} C{ij}^D =  egin{cases} 0 & \|\mathbf{p}i - \mathbf{p}j\| > d0 \  rac{\mathbf{p}i - \mathbf{p}j}{\|\mathbf{p}i - \mathbf{p}j\|} & 	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} 
abla{\mathbf{p}j} C{ij}^D =  egin{cases} 0 & \|\mathbf{p}i - \mathbf{p}j\| > d0 \  rac{\mathbf{p}j - \mathbf{p}i}{\|\mathbf{p}i - \mathbf{p}j\|} & 	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} Update position deltas. Surface particles are updated every two time steps to optimize performance. 4. Position Update: Apply the accumulated position deltas: \mathbf{p}i^{new} = 	ilde{\mathbf{p}}i + \Delta \mathbf{p}i. 5. Boundary Confinement: Confine particles to the simulation domain boundaries. 6. Velocity Update: Update velocities based on the change in position: \mathbf{v}i^{new} =  rac{\mathbf{p}i^{new} - \mathbf{p}i^{old}}{\Delta t}. Note: Vorticity confinement and XSPH correction may be disabled in 2D contexts or specific implementations. Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. import math class Vector3: def init(self, x=0.0, y=0.0, z=0.0): self.x, self.y, self.z = x, y, z def add(self, other): return Vector3(self.x + other.x, self.y + other.y, self.z + other.z) def sub(self, other): return Vector3(self.x - other.x, self.y - other.y, self.z - other.z) def mul(self, s): return Vector3(self.x s, self.y s, self.z s) def rmul(self, s): return self.mul(s) def dot(self, other): return self.x other.x + self.y other.y + self.z other.z def length(self): return math.sqrt(self.dot(self)) def normalize(self): l = self.length() return Vector3(self.x / l, self.y / l, self.z / l) if l > 1e-8 else Vector3() def getneighbors(positions, idx, kernelradius): neighbors = [] for j, p in enumerate(positions): if j != idx and (positions[idx] - p).length() <= kernelradius: neighbors.append(j) return neighbors def computekernel(p, r): q = p.length() / r if q <= 0.5: return (8.0 / (math.pi r3)) (6 q2 (q - 1) + 1) elif q <= 1.0: return (16.0 / (math.pi r3)) ((1 - q)3) return 0.0 def computekernelgradient(p, r): q = p.length() / r if q <= 0.5: return (48.0 / (math.pi r5)) (3 q - 2) p elif q <= 1.0: return -(48.0 / (math.pi r5)) q p return Vector3() def simulatepbf(particles, dt, numiterations, boundary, kernelradius, restdensity): # particles: list of dicts with keys: 'pos', 'vel', 'mass', 'oldpos', 'predictedpos', 'posdelta', 'gradnormsq' # boundary: object with 'confine(pos)' method # 1. Prediction for p in particles: acc = p.get('fext', Vector3()) / p['mass'] p['predictedpos'] = p['pos'] + dt p['vel'] + dt2 acc # 2. Constraint Solving for p in particles: p['posdelta'] = Vector3(0, 0, 0) p['gradnormsq'] = 0.0 for in range(numiterations): for i in range(len(particles)): neighbors = getneighbors([p['pos'] for p in particles], i, kernelradius) crho = -1.0 gradsumi = Vector3(0, 0, 0) for j in neighbors: diff = particles[i]['pos'] - particles[j]['pos'] gradW = computekernelgradient(diff, kernelradius) factor = particles[j]['mass'] / restdensity crho += factor computekernel(diff, kernelradius) gradsumi += factor gradW # Accumulate gradient norm squared for neighbor j gradnormjsq = factor2 gradW.dot(gradW) particles[j]['gradnormsq'] += gradnormjsq # Compute gradient norm squared for particle i gradnormisq = 0.0 for j in neighbors: diff = particles[i]['pos'] - particles[j]['pos'] gradW = computekernelgradient(diff, kernelradius) factor = particles[j]['mass'] / restdensity gradnormisq += factor2 gradW.dot(gradW) # Solve for Delta Lambda denom = dt2 (gradnormisq / particles[i]['mass'] + particles[i]['gradnormsq'] / particles[i]['mass']) alpha = 0.0 # Hard constraint for standard PBF if denom > 1e-8: deltalambda = -crho / (denom + alpha) else: deltalambda = 0.0 # Apply Delta Lambda to position deltas particles[i]['posdelta'] += (deltalambda / particles[i]['mass']) gradsumi for j in neighbors: diff = particles[i]['pos'] - particles[j]['pos'] gradW = computekernelgradient(diff, kernelradius) factor = particles[j]['mass'] / restdensity gradsumj = -factor gradW particles[j]['posdelta'] += (deltalambda / particles[j]['mass']) gradsumj # Apply accumulated deltas to positions for p in particles: p['pos'] = p['predictedpos'] + p['posdelta'] # Reset predicted position base for next iteration accumulation p['predictedpos'] = p['pos'] # 3. Boundary Confinement for p in particles: p['pos'] = boundary.confine(p['pos']) # 4. Velocity Update for p in particles: p['vel'] = (p['pos'] - p['oldpos']) / dt p['oldpos'] = p['pos'] return particles Rest Density (ho0): Target density for incompressibility. Kernel Radius (r): Determines the interaction range. Time Step (\Delta t): Small enough to satisfy CFL conditions for stability. Solver Iterations: 10 iterations for fluids, 50 for solids (in coupled systems). Distance Threshold (d0): Minimum distance for particle repulsion. 2D Contexts: Vorticity confinement and XSPH correction are disabled because cross products cannot be performed in 2D contexts. Surface Detection: Occlusion estimation requires careful threshold tuning to avoid false positives/negatives in surface particle classification. Precision: Floating-point precision issues may arise in gradient calculations for very small distances; guard against division by zero in normalization. Boundary Handling: Particles may escape the domain if boundary confinement is not applied after position updates. The XPBD simulation loop is a numerical integration method that treats a dynamic system as a set of N vertices and M constraints, converting variational energy minimization into sequential constraint projections. It distinguishes itself from standard Position-Based Dynamics (PBD) by introducing a compliance matrix \alpha, allowing constraints to be satisfied with variable stiffness rather than binary hard constraints, which enables stable simulation of both fluids and hyperelastic solids. Surface tension is applied to minimize local surface area by enforcing an area constraint on detected surface particles. Surface particles are identified via occlusion estimation: each particle is enclosed in a spherical screen, neighbors are projected onto it, and the particle is classified as surface if the cumulative projection area falls below a threshold. The normal \mathbf{n}i is computed as 	ext{normalize}(-
abla{\mathbf{p}i} C{iho}). Neighboring surface particles are triangulated on the plane perpendicular to \mathbf{n}i, and the area constraint C{iA} = \sum{t \in T(i)}  rac{1}{2} \|(\mathbf{p}{t2} - \mathbf{p}{t1}) 	imes (\mathbf{p}{t3} - \mathbf{p}{t1})\| is enforced using its Jacobians with respect to triangle vertices. A distance constraint C{ij}^D = \min\{0, \|\mathbf{p}i - \mathbf{p}j\| - d0\} is also applied to promote uniform particle distribution. Surface particles are updated every two time steps to optimize performance. Time Complexity: O(N \cdot k) per iteration, where N is the number of particles and k is the average number of neighbors. With I iterations, total time is O(I \cdot N \cdot k). Spatial hashing reduces neighbor search to O(N) on average. Memory Complexity: O(N) for particle data and neighbor lists. Measured Performance: PBF runs in real-time on modern GPUs for millions of particles. Scaling: Scales well with GPU parallelization due to independent particle updates and neighbor searches. Strengths: Stability for incompressible fluids, easy handling of surface tension, no need for pressure solvers, real-time performance. When to Use: Real-time fluid simulation, visual effects, interactive applications where stability and speed are prioritized. When Not to Use: Scientific simulations requiring high-fidelity compressible flow dynamics or complex non-Newtonian fluid behavior. Position Based Fluids (PBF) is based on Position-Based Dynamics (PBD), which was introduced by Müller et al. in 2007. PBF specifically applies PBD to fluid simulation, enforcing incompressibility via density constraints. It has been widely adopted in computer graphics for real-time fluid effects due to its stability and efficiency. Recent developments include coupling PBF with Gaussian Splatting for rendering (Gaussian Splashing). ShengzhiWu/taichi-volume-renderer, https://github.com/ShengzhiWu/taichi-volume-renderer, fetched 2026-10-26 (unofficial) Gaussian Splashing: Unified Particles for Versatile Motion Synthesis and Rendering, https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52734.2025.00057, fetched 2026-10-29

The XPBD simulation loop is a numerical integration method that treats a dynamic system as a set of N vertices and M constraints, converting variational energy minimization into sequential constraint projections. It distinguishes itself from standard Position-Based Dynamics (PBD) by introducing a compliance matrix \alpha, allowing constraints to be satisfied with variable stiffness rather than binary hard constraints, which enables stable simulation of fluids, rigid bodies, and hyperelastic objects. Inputs and Outputs Inputs: Current particle positions xn, velocities vn, external forces f{ext}(x)n, time step \Delta t, lumped mass matrix M, constraint vector C(x), compliance matrix \alpha, and solver iteration count solverIterations. Outputs: Updated positions x{n+1} and velocities v{n+1}. Data Structures Positions (x): Vector of size N (or 3N for 3D vertices). Velocities (v): Vector of size N (or 3N). Mass Matrix (M): Diagonal matrix of size N 	imes N representing lumped masses. Constraints (C): Vector of size M where each element Cj(x) is a scalar constraint value. Compliance (\alpha): Diagonal matrix of size M 	imes M where \alphaj is the compliance for the j-th constraint (inverse of stiffness). Multipliers (\lambda): Vector of size M storing constraint force multipliers. Numbered Steps 1. Predict Position: Compute the unconstrained predicted position 	ilde{x} using explicit integration with external forces. 	ilde{x} \leftarrow xn + \Delta t vn + \Delta t^2 M^{-1} f{ext}(x)n 2. Initialize Solve Variables: Set the current position estimate x0 to the predicted position 	ilde{x} and initialize multipliers \lambda0 to zero. x0 \leftarrow 	ilde{x} \lambda0 \leftarrow 0 3. Iterative Constraint Projection: Repeat for solverIterations times (index i from 0 to solverIterations - 1): a. Compute Multiplier Update (\Delta \lambda): For each constraint j, compute the update \Delta \lambdaj using a Gauss-Seidel-like scheme. \Delta \lambdaj \leftarrow  rac{-\Delta t^2 Cj(xi) - \alphaj}{\Delta t^2 
abla Cj(xi) M^{-1} 
