abla Cj M^{-1} 
abla Cj^	op is computed using the lumped mass matrix M and constraint gradients. Parameters solverIterations: Number of iterations for the XPBD solver. Set to 10 for fluids and 50 for solids. \Delta t: Simulation time step size. Set to 0.005 seconds. Edge Cases and Pitfalls Singularity: If \alphaj is zero (infinite stiffness), the denominator becomes \Delta t^2 
abla Cj M^{-1} 
abla Cj^	op. Precision: The term \Delta t^2 
abla Cj M^{-1} 
abla Cj^	op depends on mass and time step, affecting numerical stability. Boundary Conditions: The method assumes particles are free unless constrained; boundary constraints must be explicitly defined in C(x) and \alpha. Pseudocode Unverified code. The code below did not pass a test run against a reference implementation, or no test fits it, so it may contain errors. def xpbdsimulationloop(xn, vn, fext, dt, masses, constraints, alphas, solveriterations): # xn: list of positions (N,) # vn: list of velocities (N,) # fext: list of external forces (N,) # dt: float # masses: list of scalar masses (N,) # constraints: list of constraint objects with evaluate(x) and gradient(x) methods # alphas: list of compliance values (M,) # solveriterations: int N = len(xn) M = len(constraints) # Step 1: Predict Position xpred = [xn[k] + dt vn[k] + dt2 fext[k] / masses[k] for k in range(N)] # Step 2: Initialize Solve Variables xcurr = list(xpred) lambdacurr = [0.0] M # Step 3: Iterative Constraint Projection for i in range(solveriterations): deltalambda = [0.0] M deltax = [0.0] N # Compute deltalambda for each constraint for j in range(M): Cj = constraints[j].evaluate(xcurr) gradCj = constraints[j].gradient(xcurr) # list of gradients per particle # Compute denominator term: gradCj^T Minv gradCj # Sum over particles k: (1/mk) |gradCjk|^2 denomterm = sum((g2) / masses[k] for k, g in enumerate(gradCj)) # Numerator: -dt^2 Cj - alphaj numerator = - (dt 2) Cj - alphas[j] denominator = (dt 2) denomterm + alphas[j] deltalambda[j] = numerator / denominator # Compute deltax # deltax = Minv sumj (deltalambdaj gradCj) for k in range(N): sumgrad = 0.0 for j in range(M): sumgrad += deltalambda[j] constraints[j].gradient(xcurr)[k] deltax[k] = sumgrad / masses[k] # Update State for k in range(N): xcurr[k] += deltax[k] for j in range(M): lambdacurr[j] += deltalambda[j] # Step 4: Finalize Step xnplus1 = xcurr vnplus1 = [(xnplus1[k] - xn[k]) / dt for k in range(N)] return xnplus1, vnplus1 A particle-based fluid simulation method that enforces incompressibility by correcting particle positions based on density constraints, allowing real-time simulation without meshing. It uses XPBD as its underlying solver. The foundational method that XPBD extends. PBD uses hard constraints (zero compliance) for immediate satisfaction, while XPBD uses compliance for smoother, more physically accurate behavior. Strengths: The method provides an easy and efficient simulation modality, supports parallelization via a Gauss-Seidel-like scheme, and is versatile across fluids, rigid bodies, and hyperelastic objects. Limitations: Not given in the sources. When to Use: Not given in the sources. When Not to Use: Not given in the sources. XPBD was introduced as an extension of Position-Based Dynamics (PBD) to address the limitations of hard constraints in PBD, such as instability and lack of tunable stiffness. It converts the variational optimization problem of minimizing system potential energy into a series of constraint projections, making it computationally efficient and suitable for real-time applications. The method was notably used in the "Gaussian Splashing" framework for unified particle motion synthesis and rendering. Gaussian Splashing: Unified Particles for Versatile Motion Synthesis and Rendering, https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52734.2025.00057, fetched 2026-10-29

This method implements the Amanatides–Woo voxel traversal algorithm using 16-bit signed fixed-point arithmetic (Q8.8 format) to avoid floating-point operations entirely. It determines which 2D grid cells a line segment intersects by scaling coordinates and distances to integer space. It sets itself apart from its parent Integer-based Amanatides and Woo Voxel Traversal by preserving sub-cell precision through fractional bits, which prevents the rounding accumulation that occurs in pure integer DDA approaches. Inputs and Outputs: Input: start (vec2f), end (vec2f) defining the segment endpoints in floating-point grid coordinates. Output: A sequence of integer cell coordinates (x, y) passed to a GridVisitor callback via visitor.visit(x, y). Data Structures: i16: 16-bit signed integer for coordinates and deltas. u16: 16-bit unsigned integer for tMax and absDelta values. i32: 32-bit signed integer used for intermediate arithmetic to prevent overflow during division. Constants: FPSHIFT = 8, FPONE = 256 (representing 1.0 in Q8.8). Parameters: FPSHIFT: The number of fractional bits. Value: 8. FPONE: The fixed-point representation of 1.0. Value: 1 \ll 8 = 256. Steps: 1. Convert to Fixed-Point: Multiply floating-point coordinates by FPONE and cast to i16.  egin{aligned} startX{fp} &= 	ext{static\cast}<i16>(start.x 	imes FP\ONE) \ startY{fp} &= 	ext{static\cast}<i16>(start.y 	imes FP\ONE) \ endX{fp} &= 	ext{static\cast}<i16>(end.x 	imes FP\ONE) \ endY{fp} &= 	ext{static\cast}<i16>(end.y 	imes FP\ONE) \end{aligned} 2. Determine Initial and Final Cells: Shift right by FPSHIFT to get integer cell indices.  egin{aligned} x0 &= startX{fp} \gg FP\SHIFT, \quad y0 = startY{fp} \gg FP\SHIFT \ x1 &= endX{fp} \gg FP\SHIFT, \quad y1 = endY{fp} \gg FP\SHIFT \end{aligned} 3. Compute Step Directions: Determine the direction of traversal for each axis.  egin{aligned} stepX &=  egin{cases} 1 & 	ext{if } x1 > x0 \ -1 & 	ext{if } x1 < x0 \ 0 & 	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} \ stepY &=  egin{cases} 1 & 	ext{if } y1 > y0 \ -1 & 	ext{if } y1 < y0 \ 0 & 	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} \end{aligned} 4. Compute Fixed-Point Deltas: Calculate the total displacement in fixed-point.  egin{aligned} \Delta X{fp} &= endX{fp} - startX{fp} \ \Delta Y{fp} &= endY{fp} - startY{fp} \end{aligned} 5. Compute t-Deltas (Inverse Step Size): Calculate the fixed-point distance along the ray to cross one cell boundary. To avoid overflow during division, the numerator is scaled by FPONE again (effectively dividing by \Delta X{fp} in Q16.16 space, then shifting back).  egin{aligned} absDeltaX{fp} &=  egin{cases} 	ext{static\cast}<u16>(| (i32(FP\ONE) \ll FP\SHIFT) / \Delta X{fp} |) & 	ext{if } \Delta X{fp} 
eq 0 \ 	ext{max}(u16) & 	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} \ absDeltaY{fp} &=  egin{cases} 	ext{static\cast}<u16>(| (i32(FP\ONE) \ll FP\SHIFT) / \Delta Y{fp} |) & 	ext{if } \Delta Y{fp} 
eq 0 \ 	ext{max}(u16) & 	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} \end{aligned} 6. Compute Next Intersection Coordinates: Determine the fixed-point coordinate of the next grid boundary.  egin{aligned} nextX{fp} &=  egin{cases} (x0 + 1) \ll FP\SHIFT & 	ext{if } stepX > 0 \ x0 \ll FP\SHIFT & 	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} \ nextY{fp} &=  egin{cases} (y0 + 1) \ll FP\SHIFT & 	ext{if } stepY > 0 \ y0 \ll FP\SHIFT & 	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} \end{aligned} 7. Compute Initial t-Max: Calculate the fixed-point distance from the start point to the first boundary intersection.  egin{aligned} tMaxX{fp} &=  egin{cases} 	ext{static\cast}<u16>(| i32(nextX{fp} - startX{fp}) | 	imes absDeltaX{fp} \gg FP\SHIFT) & 	ext{if } \Delta X{fp} 
