Pixel Intensity Evaluation with Scene Aggregates is a method for rendering volumetric data by integrating radiance over a pixel footprint using aggregated visibility functions to handle inter-voxel correlation and background blending without order-dependent ray marching. It differs from standard ray casting by precomputing visibility terms (Aggregated Interior Visibility and Aggregated Boundary Visibility) to approximate the complex integration of scene primitives, allowing for efficient level-of-detail rendering. Inputs: Discretized scene represented as voxels. Camera parameters (pinhole assumption, box pixel reconstruction filter). Background radiance Lb (far-field assumption, independent of position). Scene primitives (e.g., truncated ellipsoids) associated with voxels. Outputs: Pixel intensity I. Data Structures: Boundary Faces: A list of 2D faces enclosing the 3D scene aggregate, determined via flood fill on the discretized scene. ABV Map: A 2D visibility map stored as Haar wavelet coefficients. Resolution: Angular resolution of 64^2 for accurate reconstruction of high-frequency silhouette signals. Parameterization: The hemispherical domain is parameterized using concentric mapping. Steps: 1. Precompute Aggregated Boundary Visibility (ABV): 1. Determine the list of boundary faces of the voxels using a flood fill iteration on the discretized scene. 2. For each boundary face, consider all directions in its inward-facing hemisphere. 3. For each direction, cast visibility rays with origins uniformly sampled on the face to estimate average visibility. 4. Use concentric mapping to parameterize the hemispherical domain and obtain a 2D visibility map. 5. Project the map to the Haar wavelet basis to store the ABV term. 2. Evaluate Pixel Intensity: 1. Define the pixel footprint P in world space. 2. Identify the subset P^+ \subseteq P such that rays originated from points in P^+ intersect the scene aggregate primitives. Points in P \setminus P^+ (denoted P^-) do not inter