u}; P^+) for the subset P^+: \hat{V}b( oldsymbol{
u}; P^+) =  rac{1}{|P|} \int{P^+} V( oldsymbol{x},  oldsymbol{
u}) d oldsymbol{x} where |P| is the area of P,  oldsymbol{
u} is the viewing direction, and V( oldsymbol{x},  oldsymbol{
u}) is the visibility at point  oldsymbol{x} in direction  oldsymbol{
u}. 4. Calculate the pixel intensity I by integrating incoming radiance Li over the footprint P: I =  rac{1}{|P|} \intP Li( oldsymbol{x}p,  oldsymbol{
u}p) d oldsymbol{x}p where  oldsymbol{
u}p is the pixel direction. 5. Evaluate Li( oldsymbol{x}p,  oldsymbol{
u}p) based on the location of  oldsymbol{x}p: If  oldsymbol{x}p \in P^-, Li( oldsymbol{x}p,  oldsymbol{
u}p) = Lb(- oldsymbol{
u}p) (background radiance). If  oldsymbol{x}p \in P^+, Li is evaluated using the aggregated visibility terms (ABV and AIV) to account for the primitive coverage and inter-voxel correlation. Parameters: Angular resolution for ABV precomputation: 64^2. Edge Cases and Pitfalls: Precision: The ABV term contains high-frequency signals responsible for reconstructing the silhouette of the scene; insufficient angular resolution may lead to inaccurate coverage reconstruction. Boundaries: The ABV term is only defined on the 2D boundary enclosing the 3D scene aggregate. The boundary must be reasonably tight such that no primitives lie outside it. Coverage Ambiguity: For points inside P^+, it may or may not be covered by the underlying geometry. The method preserves this notion of semi-transparency via primitive coverage c( oldsymbol{\omega}p). Time Complexity: Precomputation involves flood fill and ray casting for all boundary faces and directions, which is offline. Rendering time depends on the evaluation of the integral over P, utilizing precomputed wavelet coefficients. Memory Complexity: Storage of ABV and AIV terms as Haar wavelet coefficients. Nonlinear approximation and truncation can be applied to compress the visibility data. Strengths: Handles inter-voxel correlation correctly, which traditional volumetric models fail to distinguish. Enables level-of-detail rendering by aggregating scene appearance. Limitations: Requires precomputation of visibility terms. Relies on far-field assumption for background radiance. When to use: Efficient scene appearance aggregation for level-of-detail rendering where inter-voxel correlation is significant. The method was introduced in the paper "Efficient Scene Appearance Aggregation for Level-of-Detail Rendering". It addresses the limitation of traditional volumetric models that cannot distinguish between different types of inter-voxel correlation, leading to incorrect accumulation results. The approach uses aggregated visibility funct