MIP-Splatting is a rendering method for 3D Gaussian Splatting that eliminates aliasing and dilation artifacts when rendering at resolutions, focal lengths, or camera distances different from those encountered during training. It solves the mismatch between the discrete sampling of the image sensor and the continuous representation of 3D Gaussians by introducing a multi-scale representation and two specific filters: a 3D smoothing filter and a 2D Mip filter. Unlike standard 3DGS which suffers from high-frequency artifacts when zooming out and dilation effects when zooming in, MIP-Splatting constrains the representation frequency to the Nyquist limit of the training data and approximates the physical box filter of a camera sensor. Inputs: A set of 3D Gaussians \{Gk\} parameterized by center pk, covariance \Sigmak, opacity \alphak, and spherical harmonics coefficients; a camera defined by projection matrix and intrinsics; and rendering resolution. Outputs: A rendered image and visibility radii. Data Structures: Each Gaussian Gk stores: pk \in \mathbb{R}^3: Center position. \Sigmak \in \mathbb{R}^{3 	imes 3}: Covariance matrix, parameterized as \Sigmak = Ok sk sk^T Ok^T, where s \in \mathbb{R}^3 is a scaling vector and O \in \mathbb{R}^{3 	imes 3} is a rotation matrix from a quaternion. \alphak \in [0, 1]: Opacity. SHk: Spherical harmonics features. lk \in \{1, \dots, L{max}\}: Level of detail index. S{k}^{max}, S{k}^{min} \in \mathbb{R}: Maximum and minimum pixel coverage thresholds for selective rendering. Steps: 1. 3D Smoothing Filter (Training): To prevent the optimization from creating Gaussians with frequencies higher than the training data can support, a 3D low-pass filter is applied. The maximal sampling frequency \hat{
u} is derived from the training views. For a camera at depth d with focal length f, the world space sampling interval is \hat{T} = d/f, so \hat{
u} = f/d. The regularized 3D Gaussian Gk(x){reg} is the convolution of Gk with a low-pass Gaussian G{low} with variance \hat{
u}k^s \cdot I, where s is a scalar hyperparameter (typical value 0.2): Gk(x){reg} =  rac{\sqrt{|\Sigmak|}}{\sqrt{|\Sigmak + \hat{
u}k^s \cdot I|}} e^{- rac{1}{2}(x-pk)^T (\Sigmak + \hat{
u}k^s \cdot I)^{-1} (x-pk)} This effectively increases the 3D covariance: \Sigmak' = \Sigmak + \hat{
u}k^s \cdot I. The maximal sampling rate is recomputed every m iterations (typical m=100). 2. Multi-Scale Aggregation (Training): The scene is represented by Gaussians at L{max}=4 levels (1x, 4x, 16x, 64x downsampled resolution). Initialization: All Gaussians start at level l=1. Aggregation Trigger: After a warm-up stage (first 1,000 iterations), coarse-level Gaussians are created. Selection: For each coarse level lm (2 \le lm \le L{max}), render all Gaussians from levels [1, lm-1] at 4^{lm-1} times downsampled resolution. Gaussians with pixel coverage Sk < ST (threshold ST=2 pixels) are selected as "too small". Voxelization: Selected Gaussians are binned into a voxel grid of resolution (400/lm)^3. Pooling: Within each voxel, attributes (position, rotation, SH, opacity, scaling) are averaged. Enlargement: The new Gaussian's scaling is enlarged by factor ST / S{avg}, where S{avg} is the average pixel coverage of the aggregated Gaussians, targeting a pixel coverage of ST. The new Gaussian is assigned to level lm. Training: The model is trained on both original and downsampled images. 3. Selective Rendering (Inference): At render time, Gaussians are selected based on their current pixel coverage Sk relative to their stored bounds Sk^{max} and Sk^{min}. Update Bounds: If the rendering resolution scale matches the scale at which Gk was created, update bounds: Sk^{max'} = \max(\lambda1 Sk^{max}, Sk), \quad Sk^{min'} = \min(\lambda2 Sk^{min}, Sk) where \lambda1 = 0.95 and \lambda2 = 1.05. Selection Condition: A Gaussian is rendered if:  rac{Sk}{Sk^{max}} \le S{rel}^{max} \quad \land \quad \left(  rac{Sk}{Sk^{min}} \ge S{rel}^{min} \lor Sk \ge ST ight) where S{rel}^{max} = 1.5 and S{rel}^{min} = 0.5. This filters out Gaussians that are too large (causing blur) or too small (causing aliasing), except those larger than ST which are safe. 4. 2D Mip Filter (Rendering): To handle dilation artifacts when zooming in (where projected Gaussians become small but the fixed dilation k